    "knowledge_point_id": "af228cc4-8be2-5c16-8537-4e5dab4e2ff4",
    "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Tangent Curve",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/10912ded2166eb7ae28ee81c2936f008bf14eb288ec95969b822dd138be509d8.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$",
    "worked_solution": "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\pi -(-\\pi )=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n2\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ unit up. Hence, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+1.\n$$",
